  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">REDUCING CONSUMER DEMAND FOR IVORY IN CAMBODIA THROUGH STRENGTHENED CAPACITY, WILDLIFE LAW ENFORCEMENT, AND COMMUNITY ENGAGEMENT</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will support the Kouprey Express and the Wildlife Rapid Rescue Team (WRRT) to reduce threats to Asian elephants and demand for ivory in Cambodia through an approach that encompasses both wildlife law enforcement and demand reduction. The Kouprey Express Environmental Education Program empowers youth and communities to make responsible decisions regarding the sustainable management of natural resources; effect positive change; shifts in attitudes, behaviors, and actions towards wildlife and their habitat; promote pride and grassroots action to protect Asian elephants and other wildlife; and reduce consumption of ivory. The WRRT has an essential role in combating wildlife crime in Cambodia and is responsible for the majority of wildlife crime investigations in the country. This project will address threats to Asian elephants by: (1) directly combating the illegal trade in ivory through focused investigations; (2) increasing governmental and institutional capacity; (3) empowering youth and communities to make responsible decisions and shifts in attitudes, behaviors, and actions regarding the sustainable management of Cambodias wildlife and natural resources; and (4) promote pride and grassroots action to protect Asian elephants by reducing consumption for ivory. Project activities include: (1) build capacities of 750+ teachers and community leaders through three-day workshops on core environmental concepts; (2) reach 10,500+ Cambodian youth through hands-on environmental education and elephant conservation lessons, field trips to Phnom Tamao Wildlife Rescue Center, and school visits; (3) conduct door-to-door outreach and 15+ Community Night Shows in rural communities to share information about elephants, wildlife laws, and human-elephant conflict (HEC); (4) broadcast two animated public service announcements on television to promote the wildlife rescue hotline and demand reduction messages; (5) use a targeted social media campaign in Khmer, English, and</narrative>
  </description>
